# Word Search II

**LeetCode Problem:** [212. Word Search II](https://leetcode.com/problems/word-search-ii/)

## Approach: Trie + DFS + Backtracking

- First, construct a Trie using all the given words. Each Trie node stores its children, the complete word, and whether it represents the end of a word.
- Traverse every cell of the board and check whether its character exists in the Trie.
- Use DFS to explore all four adjacent directions (up, right, down, left).
- Mark the current cell as visited using `'#'` to avoid visiting the same cell again in the current path.
- Whenever a Trie node represents the end of a word, add that word to the answer and reset its `isEndOfWord` flag to avoid duplicates.
- Restore the original character during backtracking.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    struct trieNode {
        string word;
        bool isEndOfWord;
        trieNode* children[26];
    };

    trieNode* getNode() {
        trieNode* newNode = new trieNode();
        newNode->word = "";
        newNode->isEndOfWord = false;

        for(int i = 0; i < 26; i++)
            newNode->children[i] = NULL;

        return newNode;
    }

    void find_string(trieNode* node, vector<vector<char>>& board,
                     int row, int col, vector<string>& ans,
                     vector<pair<int,int>>& directions) {

        if(node->isEndOfWord == true) {
            ans.push_back(node->word);
            node->isEndOfWord = false;
        }

        char ch = board[row][col];
        board[row][col] = '#';

        for(const auto& it : directions) {
            int r = row + it.first, c = col + it.second;

            if(r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= board.size() ||
               c >= board[0].size() || board[r][c] == '#')
                continue;

            char ch = board[r][c];
            int idx = ch - 'a';

            if(node->children[idx] == NULL)
                continue;

            find_string(node->children[idx], board, r, c,
                        ans, directions);
        }

        board[row][col] = ch;
    }

    vector<string> findWords(vector<vector<char>>& board,
                             vector<string>& words) {

        trieNode* root = getNode();
        int n = words.size();

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            trieNode* node = root;

            for(char ch : words[i]) {
                int idx = ch - 'a';

                if(node->children[idx] == NULL)
                    node->children[idx] = getNode();

                node = node->children[idx];
            }

            node->word = words[i];
            node->isEndOfWord = true;
        }

        vector<string> ans;
        vector<pair<int,int>> directions = {
            {-1,0}, {0,1}, {1,0}, {0,-1}
        };

        for(int i = 0; i < board.size(); i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j < board[i].size(); j++) {
                char ch = board[i][j];
                int idx = ch - 'a';

                if(root->children[idx] == NULL)
                    continue;

                find_string(root->children[idx], board, i, j,
                            ans, directions);
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

Let:
- \(M\) = Number of rows in the board.
- \(N\) = Number of columns in the board.
- \(W\) = Number of words.
- \(L\) = Maximum length of a word.

**Time Complexity:**
- Trie Construction: \(O(W \cdot L)\)
- DFS and Backtracking: \(O(MN \cdot 3^L)\) in the worst case.

The DFS explores at most three new directions after the first move because the previous cell cannot be revisited.

**Space Complexity:**
- Trie: \(O(W \cdot L)\)
- DFS Recursion Stack: \(O(L)\)

Overall Auxiliary Space: \(O(W \cdot L + L)\), excluding the output and input board.
